# Phase banner

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A phase banner tells people that a feature, view or dashboard is still being tested. It carries a short label, such as Demo or Trial, and one sentence that says what is different and where to go for the usual version.

It is a Civica pattern. Home Assistant has no phase banner. A Markdown card with a label and a sentence is the nearest dashboard equivalent. Do not use a Home Assistant alert or warning for this: those are for faults, not for a project’s stage. The Civica theme does not add a banner to any dashboard.

This page covers:

- Phase banner
  - Civica phase banner pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

The banner has two parts on one line, which wrap on a narrow screen:

1. A tag with the label, written as text.
2. A sentence beside it that says what the label means for this task.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-phase-banner-pattern"><code>Civica phase banner pattern</code></h3>

The label is a `strong` element styled as a tag, a short coloured block. It is text, not a button or a link. The sentence follows it in the same paragraph. Put the banner at the top of the page or card it describes, above the heading.

The pattern has no theme variables of its own. For a Markdown card that plays the same role, the variables that apply to the label and text are `primary-text-color`, `info-color`, `label-badge-blue` and `label-badge-grey`. The [Tag](tag.html) page owns the label.

Use a plain label that people already understand, such as Demo or Trial. If you use alpha or beta, say what it means in the sentence beside it.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- One line sets the expectation before anyone starts a task.
- The banner takes space on every page where it appears, so remove it when the trial ends.
- It cannot report a fault, because it does not change with device state.
- A label alone is not enough. The sentence has to say what differs.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a phase banner for a clearly bounded prototype or trial that people might take for a stable feature. It does not belong on a household dashboard that is already in daily use.

Phase labels describe the real status of the project. They never suggest that an assessment or approval has taken place.

Keep the message short and the same on every page where it appears. Say whether data, actions or reliability differ from the normal version, and point to the normal route when people need it. Collect feedback through a separate, named link or the [Feedback](feedback.html) component. Do not turn the label into a button.

Remove the banner when its condition ends, and record the change in the release notes or the [page history](page-history.html). Do not use a banner for a device fault or a failed save. Those need a message in context; see [Status and notifications](status.html).

Do not announce an unchanged banner each time a sensor updates. Test long, translated messages and how the banner wraps on a narrow screen.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the words carry the meaning, not the colour of the tag.
- 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum): measure the label in your own colours before you claim a ratio.
- 3.1.3 Unusual Words: explain labels such as alpha and beta.
- 1.4.4 Resize Text and 1.4.10 Reflow: the banner wraps rather than running off the screen.

